    "created_at": "2026-09-18T23:58:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-19T00:07:39Z",
    "body": "I strongly agree with Justin here.\n\nThe thing is, it isn't the consumer of the APIs that should care about how elements/nodes handle their own state when moved.\n\nConsumers of this API (or any API that lets them move a child) just want to move the element/node.\n\nIt is the responsibility of the moved element/node itself to determine what to do, exactly like custom elements can with and without `connectedMoveCallback`.\n\nOn the other hand, native nodes throwing an error saying \"I cannot be moved\" is _totally nonsensical_ to the consumer.\n\nThis is an unnecessary leak of internal implementation detail.\n\nConsumer (even a framework's) thought process: \n\n\"I'm gonna move this node\" -> error -> \"WTF?\" -> tries other method -> it works -> \"WTF?\"\n\n(Where F stands for fudge cakes, of course 😊)\n\nIt is \n\n- not useful for this API to throw like it does\n- is error prone due to replication of internal logic needed on the outside to avoid try/catch\n- the inversion of control is the wrong way: it isn't the element/node consumer that decides if an element is movable; it is the element (or node) that decides what to do (with its own state) when moved\n- and it is inconsistent across different types of nodes.\n\nWhen we want to move a node, just let us move the node.\n\n(Parent elements that care about childList changing observe children, which is not relevant to this.)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
